@styles navbar, section, theme_head, highlight

page_title #= "What is Moth?"
page_description #= "Introduction to the Moth language and toolchain."
page_head #= theme_head

#[navbar]
#[section, $md:
    # What is Moth?
    Moth is a new programming language and toolchain.

    [highlight, $md: *This website was created using Moth!*]

    Everything you see on this page, including the layout, styles, and even the text content 
    was written in Moth files and generated using the compiler's built-in HTML project builder.

    The source code for this whole website can be found on @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/docs/src (Github).

    ## A language that makes the project builder part of compilation

    Most languages start with a general-purpose core, then leave web projects to template engines, bundlers and frameworks. 
    Moth gives project builders a first-class compiler contract. 
    
    Its HTML builder already combines typed templates, Markdown and hot reload in one toolchain. 
    Moth plans mixed JavaScript and WebAssembly output.
    That contract keeps the compiler backend-neutral, so future project builders can replace the current one and the language can remain agnostic to the platform its targetting.

    Templates are a powerful and unique workhorse feature. 
    Moth treats them as typed values that compose, support Markdown and fold inside the compiler. 
    Constant content collapses into a string before code generation while dynamic content keeps only the control flow it needs. 
    No general template engine ships with the result.

    Moth also bets on memory safety without source-level lifetime machinery. 
    Borrow validation already catches access and aliasing bugs. 
    Moth's lifetime analysis will check which values may outlive which owners, without lifetime annotations or move syntax. 
    
    Debug builds can keep GC and skip optional ownership optimisation while mandatory safety checks still run. 
    Release builds can elide the GC entierly on targets that permit it. WebAssembly being the first target with this as a goal. 

    Moth pairs that ambition with restraint. 
    It leaves out closures, operator overloading, reflection and trait objects rather than accept hidden control flow or runtime machinery without a clear role. 

    Reactivity follows the same direction. 
    Moth aims to expose sources, dependencies and sinks to the compiler instead of burying UI updates inside callbacks. 
    Project builders then get the facts for fine-grained updates, while reactive bindings and traits cover common callback patterns without a general closure system. 
    Web pages provide the first proving ground, not the final boundary.

    Moth targets developers who want a language with an opinion about what code should look like, not a catalogue of features. 
    
    This is all still in early-alpha and moving fast, but Moth will be shared more widely when the language can stand behind its promises.

    ## Documentation
    The @./docs (Alpha language docs) explains everything about the language, compiler and making Moth projects.

    This compiler is in early alpha and is undergoing rapid development, so not everything is exactly up to date with the current compiler.
    [highlight, $md: *You can already use Moth for web projects, but be aware of the early alpha status*]
]

